    chapter: "Chapter 2 — No Alarms",
    text: `The archive room smelled the way it always did, of dust and old adhesive and the particular mustiness of acid-free folders that had never quite escaped their previous decades. Wren pulled every condition report Julian had filed in his last year, laying them across the reading table in chronological order until his handwriting became a kind of second voice in the room — small, precise, faintly slanted, the letters crowded together the way a person writes when they are trying to fit more honesty into a margin than the margin was built to hold.

She compared it, on a hunch she barely trusted, to the photograph of the scratched plaque on her phone.

The letters weren’t identical — you couldn’t forge intent into a chisel stroke the same way you could into ink — but the rhythm was there. The particular way the number seventeen had been carved, with a short flat top and a long trailing seven, matched a habit she had seen a hundred times in Julian’s condition notes, where he dated entries the same idiosyncratic way.

She sat back, unsettled by how quickly certainty had arrived and by how little she wanted it to be true.

The final report in the stack, filed the week before Julian resigned, was different from the others — longer, more agitated, written in a hand that pressed hard enough to nearly tear the page. It described, in careful technical language that barely concealed the anger underneath it, an unauthorized handling session logged for Untitled Storm during a night Julian had not been scheduled to work, during which someone had removed the painting from its frame, photographed it extensively under raking light, and returned it without filing any report at all.

Someone had done that. Someone other than Julian.

He had flagged it. He had asked, in writing, for an internal review.

Wren found no record that the review had ever happened.

She sat in the archive a long time after that, the old dust settling around her, thinking that the riddle on the plaque had not been written by a man erasing a crime. It read, now, like a man documenting one — and daring the one person who read plaques twice to notice.`,
